<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-average-rates-of-change" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Average Rates of Change</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.2 Differentiation I
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>
                        Compute average and instantaneous rates of change in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Build intuition for average rate of change through a concrete motion context, and notice that the average rate depends on the interval chosen.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Do not introduce the formula until after the activity. Let learners compute the ratios by hand and notice patterns. Draw out the key observation: the stone is at the same height at <m>t=1</m> and <m>t=3</m>, so the average velocity over that interval is zero — even though the stone is clearly moving at both times.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="activity-stone-in-flight">
        <title>A Stone in Flight</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A stone is thrown upward from the ground. Its height above the ground after <m>t</m> seconds is modelled by
                <md>s(t) = 20t - 5t^2 \text{ metres.}</md>
            </p>

            <!-- TODO: TikZ figure — parabolic arc of s(t) = 20t - 5t² on [0,4], labelled with heights at t = 0,1,2,3,4, showing the stone rising and falling symmetrically about t = 2 -->

            <p>
                The table below shows the height of the stone at one-second intervals.
            </p>
            <p>
            <table xml:id="table-stone-heights">
                <title>Height of stone at each second</title>
                <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell right="minor">Time <m>t</m> (s)</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>0</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>1</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>2</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>3</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>4</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell right="minor">Height <m>s(t)</m> (m)</cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>0</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>15</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>20</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>15</m></cell>
                        <cell right="minor"><m>0</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            </p>

            <ol marker="(a)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        At what time does the stone reach its highest point? When does it land?
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            Highest point at <m>t = 2</m> s (height 20 m). Lands at <m>t = 4</m> s.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        For each time interval below, calculate
                        <md>\frac{\text{change in height}}{\text{change in time}} = \frac{s(b) - s(a)}{b - a}</md>
                        and record your answers in the table. Include units.
                    </p>

                    <table xml:id="table-stone-aroc">
                        <title>Average rate of change over different intervals</title>
                        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor">
                            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                                <cell right="minor">Start Time <m>a</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor">End Time <m>b</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor">Start Position <m>s(a)</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor">End Position <m>s(b)</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor">Average Rate of Change</cell>
                            </row>
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell right="minor"><m>0</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>1</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                            </row>
                            <!-- <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell right="minor"><m>0</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>2</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                            </row>
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell right="minor"><m>0</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>4</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                            </row> -->
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell right="minor"><m>1</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>2</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                            </row>
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell right="minor"><m>2</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>3</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                            </row>
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell right="minor"><m>3</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>4</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                            </row>
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell right="minor"><m>0</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>4</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                            </row>
                        </tabular>
                    </table>

                    <!-- <table xml:id="table-stone-aroc">
                        <title>Average rate of change over different intervals</title>
                        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor">
                            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                                <cell right="minor">Interval <m>[a,b]</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>[0,4]</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>[0,2]</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>[0,1]</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>[1,2]</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>[2,3]</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>[3,4]</m></cell>
                            </row>
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell right="minor">Average rate (m/s)</cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                                <cell right="minor"><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                            </row>
                        </tabular>
                    </table> -->

                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            <m>[0,1]</m>: <m>15</m> m/s; <m>[1,2]</m>: <m>5</m> m/s; <m>[2,3]</m>: <m>-5</m> m/s; <m>[3,4]</m>: <m>-15</m> m/s; <m>[0,4]</m>: <m>0</m> m/s
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The average rate over <m>[0,4]</m> is <m>0</m> m/s. Does this mean the stone was stationary the whole time? Explain.
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            No<mdash/>the stone rises and falls, returning to its starting height. The average rate is zero because the net displacement is zero, not because the stone wasn't moving.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                    <note component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            This is a crucial point: the <em>average</em> rate of change over an interval can be misleading about what happens <em>within</em> that interval. This motivates the need for an <em>instantaneous</em> rate of change — the main goal of the chapter.
                        </p>
                    </note>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What does a negative average rate mean in this context? What do you notice about the rates over <m>[1,2]</m> and <m>[2,3]</m>?
                    </p>
                    <solution component="lesson-plan">
                        <p>
                            A negative rate means the stone is falling. The rates over <m>[1,2]</m> and <m>[2,3]</m> are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign (<m>+5</m> and <m>-5</m> m/s), reflecting the symmetry of the parabola about its peak at <m>t = 2</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Suggested Time</title>
                    <p>
                        5 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        Formalise the ratio learners computed in the activity as the average rate of change formula, and introduce the geometric interpretation as the slope of the secant line.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Connect to the activity:</alert> every entry in the table was computed using <m>\frac{s(b)-s(a)}{b-a}</m>. This ratio is the average rate of change. It is also the slope of the straight line joining two points on the curve — called the <term>secant line</term>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The AROC depends on the interval: different choices of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> give different values. This is fundamentally different from the slope of a straight line, which is the same everywhere.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Introduce the <m>h</m>-notation: writing the second point as <m>a + h</m> (where <m>h</m> is the gap between the two inputs) gives the compact formula <m>\text{AROC} = \frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <exploration>        
        <p>
        The interactive graph below shows the stone's path <md>s(t) = 20t - 5t^2</md> Drag the two points <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> along the curve to choose an interval <m>[a, b]</m>. The green secant line joins them, and the readout shows the run <m>\Delta t</m>, the rise <m>\Delta s</m>, and the average rate of change <m>\Delta s / \Delta t</m>. Try to reproduce the rows of your table — and notice that the interval from <m>t = 1</m> to <m>t = 3</m> gives an average rate of zero, even though the stone is moving at both times.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive-secant-average-rate" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="6:5" source="jsxgraph/secant-average-rate.js">
            <slate xml:id="secant-average-rate" surface="jsxboard" aspect="6:5"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>Drag <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> along the curve. The slope of the green secant, <m>\Delta s / \Delta t</m>, is the average rate of change over the interval <m>[a, b]</m>.</p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
        
    </exploration>

    <insight xml:id="insight-aroc-key-ideas">
        <title>Average Rate of Change and Secant Lines</title>
        <p>
            The ratio computed in the activity<mdash/>change in output divided by change in input<mdash/>is the <term>average rate of change</term> of the function over that interval. Geometrically, it is the slope of the <term>secant line</term> connecting the two points on the curve.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>The average rate of change depends on the interval chosen.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>A positive AROC means the function is increasing on average; a negative AROC means it is decreasing on average.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>A zero AROC does not mean the function is constant — only that it ends at the same value it started.</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </insight>

    <p>
        Consider a function <m>f</m> and two points on its graph: <m>(a, f(a))</m> and a second point to the right at <m>(a+h, f(a+h))</m> for some <m>h \gt 0</m>, as shown in <xref ref="fig-secant-line"/>. The straight line connecting them is called the <term>secant line</term>. Its slope is
        <md>\frac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{(a+h) - a} = \frac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}</md>
        and this is the average rate of change of <m>f</m> between <m>x = a</m> and <m>x = a+h</m>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-secant-line">
        <caption>
            The secant line through <m>(a, f(a))</m> and <m>(a+h, f(a+h))</m>.
        </caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-secant-line" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="6:5" source="jsxgraph/secant-line.js">
            <slate xml:id="secant-line" surface="jsxboard" aspect="6:5"/>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <insight xml:id="insight-aroc-formula">
        <title>Average Rate of Change</title>
        <p>
            The <term>average rate of change</term> (AROC) of a function <m>f</m> over the interval from <m>x = a</m> to <m>x = a + h</m> is
            <md>\text{AROC} = \frac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}.</md>
            This equals the slope of the secant line joining <m>(a,\, f(a))</m> and <m>(a+h,\, f(a+h))</m> on the graph of <m>f</m>.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-aroc-quadratic">
        <title>Average Rate of Change of a Quadratic</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Find the average rate of change of <m>f(x) = x^2 + 1</m> from <m>x = 2</m> to <m>x = 5</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Here <m>a = 2</m> and <m>a + h = 5</m>, so <m>h = 3</m>.
                <md>
                    <mrow>\text{AROC} \amp= \frac{f(5) - f(2)}{3} = \frac{26 - 5}{3} = \frac{21}{3} = 7</mrow>
                </md>
                The function increases by an average of <m>7</m> units per unit increase in <m>x</m> over this interval.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-aroc-freefall">
        <title>Average Velocity of a Falling Object</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                An object falls from rest. Its distance below the starting point after <m>t</m> seconds is <m>d(t) = 5t^2</m> metres.
                Find its average velocity between <m>t = 2</m> and <m>t = 4</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\text{AROC} \amp= \frac{d(4) - d(2)}{4 - 2} </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \frac{5(16) - 5(4)}{2}  </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \frac{80 - 20}{2} </mrow> 
                    <mrow> \amp = 30 \text{ m/s}</mrow>
                </md>
                The object falls at an average speed of <m>30</m> m/s over this two-second interval.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-aroc-profit">
        <title>Average Rate of Change of Profit</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A small business models its monthly profit (in thousands of KSh) from selling <m>x</m> units as
                <md>P(x) = -x^2 + 80x - 400.</md>
                Find the average rate of change of profit as production increases from <m>20</m> to <m>40</m> units, and interpret your answer.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>P(20) \amp= -(20)^2 + 80(20) - 400 </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = -400 + 1600 - 400 </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 800</mrow>
                    <mrow>P(40) \amp= -(40)^2 + 80(40) - 400 </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = -1600 + 3200 - 400 </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 1200</mrow>
                    <mrow> \text{AROC} \amp = \frac{P(40) - P(20)}{40 - 20} </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \frac{1200 - 800}{20} </mrow> 
                    <mrow> \amp = 20,000 \text{ KSh per unit} </mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                On average, each additional unit produced (between 20 and 40 units) increases monthly profit by KSh 20 000.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-aroc">

        <exercise xml:id="ex-aroc-car">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A car's distance from Nairobi (in km) after <m>t</m> hours is modelled by <m>s(t) = 3t^2</m>.
                    Find the car's average speed between <m>t = 1</m> and <m>t = 3</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>12</m> km/h</p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="PRECALC-wWwvbN2t0s-parts" original="PRECALC/modules/m49455/index.cnxml" license="CC-BY-4.0">
            <statement>
                <p>For each pair of points, find the average rate of change of the function whose graph passes through them.</p>
                <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                    <li>
                        <m>(-2,\, 0)</m> and <m>(-4,\, 5)</m>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <m>(4,\, -3)</m> and <m>(-2,\, -1)</m>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <m>(0,\, 5)</m> and <m>(6,\, 5)</m>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <m>(7,\, -2)</m> and <m>(7,\, 10)</m>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                    <li><m>-\dfrac{5}{2}</m></li>
                    <li><m>-\dfrac{1}{3}</m></li>
                    <li><m>0</m></li>
                    <li>Undefined (vertical line)</li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="PRECALC-Iv1ayoeSJv-parts" original="PRECALC/modules/m49455/index.cnxml" license="CC-BY-4.0">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The revenue (in KSh) generated by selling <m>x</m> items is <m>R(x) = 2x^2 + 10x</m>.
                    Find the average rate of change of revenue as <m>x</m> increases from <m>10</m> to <m>20</m> items.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\text{AROC} \amp= \frac{R(20) - R(10)}{10} </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{(800+200) - (200+100)}{10} </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \frac{1000 - 300}{10} </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 70 \text{ KSh per item}</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-aroc-cost">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The cost (in KSh) of producing <m>x</m> bags of maize flour is <m>C(x) = x^2 - 4x + 1000</m>.
                    Find the average rate of change in cost as production increases from <m>x = 10</m> to <m>x = 15</m> bags.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>KSh 21 per bag</p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-aroc-expression-in-h">
            <title>AROC as an Algebraic Expression</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f(x) = x^2</m>.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the average rate of change of <m>f</m> between <m>x = a</m> and <m>x = a + h</m>. Simplify your answer as far as possible.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>What value does the AROC approach as <m>h \to 0</m>? What do you think this limiting value represents?</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p> We have:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\text{AROC} \amp= \frac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h} = \frac{(a+h)^2 - a^2}{h}</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= \frac{a^2 + 2ah + h^2 - a^2}{h} = \frac{2ah + h^2}{h} = 2a + h</mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            As <m>h \to 0</m>, the AROC <m>= 2a + h \to 2a</m>. This limiting value is the slope of the tangent to <m>f(x) = x^2</m> at <m>x = a</m>, the instantaneous rate of change at that point.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-aroc-reverse">
            <title>Working Backwards</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The average rate of change of <m>f(x) = x^2</m> between <m>x = 2</m> and <m>x = b</m>, where <m>b \neq 2</m>, is equal to <m>9</m>.
                    Find the value of <m>b</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\frac{f(b) - f(2)}{b - 2} \amp= 9</mrow>
                        <mrow>\frac{b^2 - 4}{b - 2} \amp= 9</mrow>
                        <mrow>\frac{(b-2)(b+2)}{b-2} \amp= 9</mrow>
                        <mrow>b + 2 \amp= 9</mrow>
                        <mrow>b \amp= 7</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

